WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.350
Who decides all this?

2
00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:07.525
Book 08's Chapter 09 gives one decider per
row, and separates deciding from doing.

3
00:00:07.525 --> 00:00:12.102
The sponsor and CIO decide the
platform and its deployment model,

4
00:00:12.102 --> 00:00:16.002
with the DNS/DDI team doing
the evaluation and the CISO,

5
00:00:16.002 --> 00:00:20.325
the authorizing official and
the operating teams consulted.

6
00:00:20.325 --> 00:00:25.175
They also decide how divisions are scoped,
and break ties over the resolver path.

7
00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:30.306
The authorizing official decides whether
a cloud-delivered management plane is

8
00:00:30.306 --> 00:00:34.607
authorized for the agency's use,
and accepts the standing exceptions:

9
00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:37.700
an internal zone that loses
DNSSEC in its new home,

10
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.850
or an encrypted DNS exception that
has outlived the agency's limit.

11
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.385
The CISO and ISSO set
the control requirements:

12
00:00:45.385 --> 00:00:50.335
DNSSEC signing of public zones and
validation on agency resolvers,

13
00:00:50.335 --> 00:00:55.550
encrypted DNS policy, and Protective
DNS forwarding and query logging.

14
00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:01.579
The DNS/DDI team decides the namespace and
the delegation of zones to divisions and

15
00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:05.948
applications, owns the address
plan and its top-level blocks,

16
00:01:05.948 --> 00:01:10.055
and approves high-impact changes:
zones, NS and DS records,

17
00:01:10.055 --> 00:01:13.637
forwarding rules, wildcards
and response policy.

18
00:01:13.637 --> 00:01:18.510
Standard record changes inside a division's
delegated namespace are pre-approved,

19
00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.476
so the division or application
owner does the work

20
00:01:21.476 --> 00:01:24.300
through a scoped account,
without a change board.

21
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.426
That team holds the most
powerful roles in the design,

22
00:01:27.426 --> 00:01:31.688
so the book scopes its automation, puts
a second approver in front of zone,

23
00:01:31.688 --> 00:01:33.819
delegation and forwarding changes,

24
00:01:33.819 --> 00:01:37.580
and keeps its privileged roles
eligible rather than standing.
